Expected Outcomes
A working Maven project that demonstrates the Splitter, Aggregator, and Recipient List EIPs using Apache Camel
Console output showing a single generated order being split into four items, individually processed, aggregated back into one order with tax applied, and dynamically dispatched to multiple downstream routes
A working understanding of how correlationId headers and completionSize/completionTimeout control aggregation, and how recipientList enables conditional, dynamic routing
Troubleshooting
Aggregation never completes / no "Aggregation completed" log appears: Check that completionSize(4) matches the actual number of items produced by the splitter, and confirm the correlationId header is being set consistently before the message reaches direct:aggregateItems.
mvn exec:java fails with "No plugin found": Confirm the exec-maven-plugin is present in the pom.xml <build><plugins> section and that you ran mvn clean compile first so classes exist to execute.
Conclusion
In this lab, you built a multi-route Apache Camel application that implements three foundational Enterprise Integration Patterns end to end: the Splitter, which broke a composite order into individually processable items; the Aggregator, which used a correlation header and a custom AggregationStrategy with a completion size and timeout to reassemble those items into a finalized order; and the Recipient List, which dynamically dispatched the finalized order to a variable set of downstream routes based on runtime business conditions such as order total. Along the way you saw how headers propagate correlation state across routes, how streaming and completion conditions govern aggregator behavior, and how a resolver method can drive dynamic, conditional routing without hardcoding destinations into the route definition. As a next step, you could scale this design by replacing the direct endpoints with real messaging transports such as JMS or Kafka, persist aggregation state for recovery across restarts, and add error handling with dead letter channels to make the pipeline resilient under production load.
